>How to Use This Tutorial</SectionComponent>
<p>This tutorial consists of <strong>nine lessons</strong>, each covering a specific topic while building upon the previous ones.<br>
For the best learning experience, follow the lessons in the given order.</p>
<p>The easiest way to follow along is to use the <em>create project</em> command and choose the language <code>Education</code>.<br>
This lets you recreate the example project and experiment with its components.</p>
<pre class="language-bash"><code class="language-bash">npm create freon@latest</code></pre>
<p>In the <code>Education</code> project, you’ll find all definition files for each lesson in separate folders.<br>
This prevents you from being distracted by details not yet introduced.<br>
For example, the files for Lesson 3 are in the folder <code>lesson3-defs</code>, and so on.<br>
Lesson 1 files are located in the <code>src/defs</code> folder (see <a
  href="/Tutorial/Overview#where-do-the-files-go-4"
>Where do the files go?</a>).</p>
<p>Before starting a new lesson, empty the <code>src/defs</code> folder and copy in the files for the lesson you’re about to follow.<br>
You’ll also find example models for the lessons in the <strong>modelstore</strong> folder.<br>
(For details on the project contents, see <a
  href="/Documentation/Overview/Getting_Started#project-structure-5"
>Project Structure</a>.)<br>
Use the model <code>Lesson1</code> for Lessons 1–3, <code>Lesson4</code> for Lessons 4–5, and <code>Lesson6</code> for the remaining lessons.</p>
<p>For tips on using the editor, click the <strong>question mark</strong> icon on the editor’s top bar.</p>
<SectionComponent tag="h2" 
  id="where-do-the-files-go-4"
  bind:intersecting={visibleById["where-do-the-files-go-4"]}
  onIntersectChange={(visible) => setVisible("where-do-the-files-go-4", visible)}
>Where Do the Files Go?</SectionComponent>
<p>Your project is set up so that all Freon definition files reside in the <code>src/defs</code> folder.<br>
If you prefer a different structure, you can modify your <code>package.json</code> file.<br>
Check the scripts for <code>generate</code> and <code>clean-gen</code>, which reference <code>src/defs</code>.<br>
Changing these paths allows you to place your language definition files anywhere you like.
You might also adjust the output location (currently <code>src/freon</code>).</p>
<pre class=""><code class="">"generate": "freon -v all -d src/defs -o src/freon",
"clean-gen": "freon clean-it -d src/defs -o src/freon",</code></pre>
<p>In this tutorial, we’ll create several Freon definition files.<br>
Note that the <strong>file extension</strong> (e.g., <code>.ast</code>, <code>.valid</code>) is important, while the file name is not.<br>
All metamodel definitions belong in <code>.ast</code> files, editor definitions in <code>.edit</code> files, and so on.</p>
<p>If you’re following this tutorial in your own IDE and notice that the browser occasionally displays an empty page after regeneration, don’t worry.<br>
This happens because the browser reloads faster than the regeneration process completes.<br>
In the browser’s developer console, you might see the message:
<code>Uncaught SyntaxError: Unexpected end of input</code>.
Simply reload the page—everything will display correctly afterward.</p>


